WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.800
Structured outputs sound like a blank check, ask for JSON and always get valid JSON.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:11.300
But the guarantee only holds when your schema fits a supported subset.

3
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:14.800
And a few constraints you write might never reach it at all.

4
00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:21.399
This lesson maps what works, what doesn't, and where the promise bends. Let's dig in.

5
00:00:21.399 --> 00:00:24.500
Structured outputs makes a strong promise.

6
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.500
And that promise has an edge to it.

7
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.299
Everything rests on the enforceable subset.

8
00:00:29.299 --> 00:00:34.799
The slice of JSON's schema, the grammar engine can actually compile and guarantee

9
00:00:34.799 --> 00:00:37.799
during generation, through constrained decoding.

10
00:00:37.799 --> 00:00:42.799
Write a keyword outside that subset and the raw API returns a 400 error,

11
00:00:42.799 --> 00:00:45.799
but the official SDK transforms it for you.

12
00:00:45.799 --> 00:00:49.799
It strips the keyword, folds its intent into the field's description,

13
00:00:49.799 --> 00:00:53.799
and validates the final response against your original full schema.

14
00:00:53.799 --> 00:00:55.799
Then there are the guarantee gaps.

15
00:00:55.799 --> 00:01:01.299
Two situations break the schema promise entirely, and both are expected behavior.

16
00:01:01.299 --> 00:01:03.799
Not bugs you should retry blindly.

17
00:01:03.799 --> 00:01:07.800
Everything else in this lesson hangs off those pieces.

18
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.800
Let's start with the good news, the supported typeset.

19
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.300
All of the standard JSON types are here.

20
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:18.300
You've got the structural types, object and array, which let you nest and group.

21
00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:23.300
You've got the scalar types, string, integer, number, boolean, and null.

22
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:29.800
And you've got enum and const, which pin a field to a fixed set of allowed values or a single constant.

23
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:33.800
That combination covers most real extraction and classification work.

24
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:37.800
If you're pulling fields out of a document or sorting something into categories,

25
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.800
these types alone usually carry the whole schema.

26
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.800
So the foundation is solid.

27
00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:47.800
Where it gets interesting is the layer above the basic types, composition.

28
00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:52.300
Composition is how you build richer shapes, and there are a few pieces to know.

29
00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:55.800
Any of lets the model pick one branch from several.

30
00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:59.800
That's how you express a union, where a field could be one shape or another.

31
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:03.800
All of combines constraints together, but watch the catch.

32
00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:06.800
All of paired with a reference isn't supported.

33
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:08.800
So don't lean on that pattern.

34
00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:10.800
Then you've got references.

35
00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:17.881
Internal ones, using $ref and $defs, let you define a shape once and reuse it across the schema,

36
00:02:17.881 --> 00:02:19.726
which keeps things clean.

37
00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:25.300
But external references, the kind that point at a remote URL, are rejected outright.

38
00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:28.300
So compose freely inside your own document.

39
00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:31.300
Just keep every reference local.

40
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:36.300
Two smaller features round out what's supported, and both are narrow but genuinely useful.

41
00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:38.300
First, string formats.

42
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:45.300
You can constrain a string to a shape like DateTime, PlainDate, Email, a URI, or a UUID.

43
00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:50.300
That's handy when you need a field to look like a real timestamp or identifier.

44
00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:52.300
Second, ArrayMinItems.

45
00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:59.300
You can require an array to be non-empty, but only barely, because the only supported values are 0 and 1.

46
00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.300
You can't say, give me at least 5.

47
00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:04.300
And RegX is supported at a basic level.

48
00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:07.800
Character classes, groups, and simple quantifiers.

49
00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:14.411
Anything richer than these falls outside the compiled grammar, which brings us to the full picture

50
00:03:14.411 --> 00:03:16.232
of enforced versus ignored.

51
00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:20.300
Here's the line that trips people up most, so let's draw it clearly.

52
00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:25.300
On the grammar-enforceable side, constrained decoding guarantees what the model can emit.

53
00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:33.689
That's your types, enum, const, anyof, allof, internal references, string formats, minItems of 0 or

54
00:03:33.689 --> 00:03:35.215
1, and basic RegX.

55
00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:38.300
All of those really constrain generation.

56
00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:40.300
Everything else is the other bucket.

57
00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:44.300
Numeric bounds, minimum, maximum, and multiple of.

58
00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:48.300
String length, minLength, and maxLength. MaxItems.

59
00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:51.300
And additional properties, unless it's set to false.

60
00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:55.300
Send any of these to the raw API, and you get a 400 error.

61
00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:57.800
The official SDK handles it instead.

62
00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:04.466
It strips the keyword, folds its intent into the field description, and validates the final response

63
00:04:04.466 --> 00:04:06.733
against your original full schema.

64
00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:10.800
A few more constructs fall outside the grammar the same way.

65
00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:16.799
Recursive schemas, the kind that reference themselves to describe a tree or a nested comment thread.

66
00:04:16.799 --> 00:04:21.799
External references, pointing at a remote URL instead of a local definition.

67
00:04:21.799 --> 00:04:28.799
And complex RegX features, back references, lookAhead and lookBehind, and word boundaries.

68
00:04:28.799 --> 00:04:32.799
Send any of these to the raw API, and you get a 400 error.

69
00:04:32.799 --> 00:04:35.799
Exactly like the numeric and length keywords.

70
00:04:35.799 --> 00:04:39.799
The official SDK transforms them for you instead.

71
00:04:39.799 --> 00:04:42.799
But the practical move is to design them out from the start.

72
00:04:42.799 --> 00:04:45.799
Flatten a recursive structure into a bounded one.

73
00:04:45.799 --> 00:04:48.799
Inline your references, and keep patterns simple.

74
00:04:48.799 --> 00:04:53.799
That gives you a clean grammar, rather than leaning on the transform to bail you out.

75
00:04:53.799 --> 00:04:56.799
Next, the hard numeric limits.

76
00:04:56.799 --> 00:05:00.799
And these are worth memorizing, because they're easy to hit at scale.

77
00:05:00.799 --> 00:05:06.799
First, at most, 20 tools can carry strict mode in a single request.

78
00:05:06.799 --> 00:05:09.799
Non-strict tools don't count against that.

79
00:05:09.799 --> 00:05:11.799
Second, optional parameters.

80
00:05:11.799 --> 00:05:19.029
Across every strict schema and structured output in the request, you get a combined budget of just

81
00:05:19.029 --> 00:05:20.726
24 optional parameters.

82
00:05:20.799 --> 00:05:26.799
That's a shared pool, so a handful of tools with 6 optional fields apiece already maxes it out.

83
00:05:26.799 --> 00:05:29.799
Third, union-typed parameters.

84
00:05:29.799 --> 00:05:36.799
Anything typed through any of, or a type array, like string or null, is capped at 16 total.

85
00:05:36.799 --> 00:05:43.080
The thing to notice is that these are request-wide budgets, not per tool, so complexity adds up

86
00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:44.733
faster than you'd expect.

87
00:05:44.799 --> 00:05:48.799
You met these two guarantee gaps in the structured outputs lesson.

88
00:05:48.799 --> 00:05:50.799
Here is what each one actually returns.

89
00:05:50.799 --> 00:05:53.799
The first is a refusal.

90
00:05:53.799 --> 00:06:00.799
If the request triggers a safety response, the model returns a refusal message, and safety wins over the schema.

91
00:06:00.799 --> 00:06:07.799
You'll see a stop reason of refusal, a normal 200 status, and build tokens, but the body won't match your shape.

92
00:06:07.799 --> 00:06:10.799
The second is a max-tokens cutoff.

93
00:06:10.799 --> 00:06:14.799
If generation hits the token ceiling, the output truncates.

94
00:06:14.799 --> 00:06:16.799
Don't assume it's still valid JSON.

95
00:06:16.799 --> 00:06:22.735
A truncated response can be incomplete and can fail to parse, because the closing braces and

96
00:06:22.735 --> 00:06:24.735
brackets may never get omitted.

97
00:06:24.799 --> 00:06:29.799
The fix is to check for a max-tokens stop reason and retry with a higher limit.

98
00:06:29.799 --> 00:06:35.799
In both cases, the first step is the same, check the stop reason before you trust the payload.

99
00:06:35.799 --> 00:06:39.799
Last piece, and it's about performance, not correctness.

100
00:06:39.799 --> 00:06:43.799
Your schema doesn't run as is, it compiles into a grammar first.

101
00:06:43.799 --> 00:06:46.799
That compile has real-timing behavior worth knowing.

102
00:06:46.799 --> 00:06:53.799
The very first request against a brand new schema pays a one-time compile latency, so that call feels slower.

103
00:06:53.799 --> 00:07:01.799
After that, the compiled grammar is cached for 24 hours from its last use, and repeat requests are much faster.

104
00:07:01.799 --> 00:07:03.799
Now, what busts the cache?

105
00:07:03.799 --> 00:07:09.799
Changing the schema's structure does, and so does changing the set of tools in the request.

106
00:07:09.799 --> 00:07:10.799
But here's a nice detail.

107
00:07:10.799 --> 00:07:15.799
Renaming a field or editing a description alone doesn't force a recompile.

108
00:07:15.799 --> 00:07:19.799
So cosmetic tweaks stay cheap, structural ones don't.

109
00:07:20.799 --> 00:07:23.799
The failure here doesn't look like a crash.

110
00:07:23.799 --> 00:07:27.799
It looks like a validator you wrote that quietly stopped being enforced,

111
00:07:27.799 --> 00:07:32.799
because the keyword you leaned on never made it to the grammar in the first place.

112
00:07:32.799 --> 00:07:36.799
Everything inside the supported subset really is guaranteed.

113
00:07:36.799 --> 00:07:39.799
Everything outside is just a suggestion.

114
00:07:39.799 --> 00:07:44.799
Just remember, a refusal or a truncated response breaks the promise, too.

115
00:07:44.799 --> 00:07:47.799
So read the stop reason before you parse.

116
00:07:47.799 --> 00:07:50.799
That wraps up this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

